.TH FID 2
.SH NAME
fid_bind, fid_lookup, fid_open, fid_is_open, fid_release, walk, attach, node_of, open_node, default_reply \- a server's fid table and static tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "Fid_Table :: struct { slots: [16]Fid_Slot, lock: Spin }"
.B "fid_lookup :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid) -> i32"
.B "fid_bind :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid, node: i32) -> bool"
.B "fid_open :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid) -> bool"
.B "fid_is_open :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid) -> bool"
.B "fid_release :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid)"
.PP
.B "Step :: #type proc(from: i32, name: string) -> i32"
.B "Qid_Of :: #type proc(node: i32) -> vectra9.Qid"
.B "walk :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, m: vectra9.Twalk, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, step: Step, qid_of: Qid_Of)"
.B "attach :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, m: vectra9.Tattach, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, root: i32, qid_of: Qid_Of)"
.B "node_of :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid, reply: ^vectra9.Msg) -> (i32, bool)"
.B "open_node :: proc(t: ^Fid_Table, fid: vectra9.Fid, reply: ^vectra9.Msg) -> (i32, bool)"
.B "default_reply :: proc(request: ^vectra9.Msg, reply: ^vectra9.Msg) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B Fid_Table
is the table a userland 9P server binds client fids into, holding up to
sixteen at once.
Each server's own tree is a small integer, the
.IR node ,
and what a node number means is the server's business; the table only
maps a fid to one.
.PP
.I Fid_lookup
answers the node a fid is bound to, or \-1 for a fid this server never
saw.
.I Fid_bind
points
.I fid
at
.IR node ,
reusing the slot if
.I fid
is already bound, since 9P allows a client to walk a fid onto itself.
It answers
.B false
when the table is full.
.I Fid_open
marks a fid as opened by the client, and
.I fid_is_open
reports it; 9P forbids a walk on an open fid and a read of one that is
not.
.I Fid_release
forgets a fid, silently if this server never held it.
.PP
.I Walk
answers a
.B Twalk
over the tree
.I step
and
.I qid_of
describe.
.I Step
takes a node and a name and answers another node, or \-1.
.I Qid_of
names a node on the wire.
A walk that gets partway answers with the qids it managed and does not
bind
.IR newfid ;
only a walk that fails on the first name is an error, which is the
partial-walk rule 9P requires.
.PP
.I Attach
binds a client's first fid, in
.IR m.fid ,
to
.IR root .
It is the one place a fid appears from nothing; every other fid in a
session comes from a walk off one of these.
.PP
.I Node_of
resolves
.IR m.fid ,
and sets
.I reply
to
.B EBADF
when the fid is not bound.
.I Open_node
is the same, and also refuses with
.B EINVAL
a fid the client has not opened, for a
.B Tread
or
.BR Treaddir .
Both are meant to be checked in one line:
.IP
.EX
node, ok := libuser.node_of(&fids, m.fid, reply)
if !ok {
	return
}
.EE
.PP
.I Default_reply
sets
.I reply
before a handler's switch runs, and reports whether the switch should
run at all.
A message that would create, rename or link a file answers
.B EPERM
without reaching the switch, because none of these servers create files.
Anything else answers
.B EOPNOTSUPP
unless a case in the switch overwrites it, so a message nobody wrote a
case for is refused rather than answered with whatever the reply buffer
last held.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Ramfs (4)'s
handler is the shape every server here shares:
.IP
.EX
if !libuser.default_reply(request, reply) {
	return
}
#partial switch m in request^ {
case vectra9.Tattach:
	libuser.attach(&fids, m, reply, NODE_ROOT, qid_of)
case vectra9.Twalk:
	libuser.walk(&fids, m, reply, step, qid_of)
case vectra9.Tlopen:
	node, ok := libuser.node_of(&fids, m.fid, reply)
	if !ok {
		return
	}
	libuser.fid_open(&fids, m.fid)
	reply^ = vectra9.Rlopen{qid = qid_of(node), iounit = 0}
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/fid.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR serve (2),
.IR 9p (2),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR walk (5),
.IR attach (5),
.IR error (5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Walk ,
.I attach ,
.I node_of
and
.I open_node
set an
.B Rlerror
into
.I reply
and answer
.B false
on any failure; nothing here panics on a client's mistake.
.SH BUGS
Sixteen fids is fixed, and a server that needs more answers
.B ENFILE
rather than growing the table: a table a client can grow is a table a
client can exhaust the machine through.
